WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.290
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.549
that teaches you to think like an engineer.

00:00:06.549 --> 00:00:12.490
Introduction: Opening, scene in a pub listening
to a song and opening the shazam app.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.470
Maybe be tricky to film.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.949
What you just witnessed was the Shazam app
recognising a song in a noisy environment,

00:00:18.949 --> 00:00:23.859
and proceeding to find a match for it among
the millions of songs in its servers database.

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:26.519
For most this probably seems like a trivial
task.

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:31.119
Our brains can identify songs incredibly quickly
from a young age, but the pathways in your

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:36.210
brain that allow you to identify a song quickly
are incredibly complex.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.270
Often times you simply need to hear just a
few chords to know exactly what song is about

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:45.120
to play, that jolt of excitement when you
can hear a DJ fading in the baseline of your

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.360
favourite song.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.230
A simple combination of tones in a specific
order allow you to identify a song from the

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:56.260
thousands of other songs you have heard in
your life in an instant, but coding a computer

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.910
do the same thing is an incredible challenge.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:02.360
A computer does not have an intuitive understanding
of music.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.979
A computer can only compare songs to other
songs in its database, looking for a match

00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:08.360
by comparison.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.770
It is a problem akin to finding a needle in
a haystack, where you can only find the needle

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:17.930
by looking at a picture of a needle and comparing
it to each individual straw, comparing it’s

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.869
length and colour until you finally find the
needle.

00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:25.030
To create a software capable of doing this
task quickly poses a very interesting coding

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:29.780
challenge and the solution the engineers at
Shazam came up with gives us some interesting

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.530
insight into how our own brains work.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.940
A study by the Manchester Museum of Science
and Industry tested 12,000 people’s ability

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.040
to recognise a song.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.859
They created an interactive game to search
for the most recognisable songs, where they

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:47.639
would play the hook of 1000 best selling songs
and recorded the time required to identify

00:01:47.639 --> 00:01:48.639
them.[1]

00:01:48.639 --> 00:01:53.950
Can you identify this song with just 2.3 seconds
of the hook?

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:58.189
That was the Spice Girls song Wannabe, which
ranks highest with a recognition time averaging

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:02.990
just 2.3 seconds, and that’s including the
reaction time required to hit the button.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:06.380
Our brains are hardwired for this kind of
pattern recognition.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.069
In a world where recognising the sound of
an approaching threat meant life or death,

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:14.500
we have evolved incredibly efficient ways
of categorizing and accessing historical data

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.710
like this.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:19.340
Our brain does not take the sound and compare
it to every sound we have ever heard like

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:25.159
a computer, the specific combination of chords
in progression simply activates specific neurons

00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:27.540
that unlock that historical data.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.209
What if the chords were played by a different
instrument?

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:32.510
Would we recognise the song as quickly?

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:38.959
Those same 2.3 seconds played on a guitar
sounds like this.

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:42.409
The notes are exactly the same, but they don’t
sound the exactly the same.

00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:45.439
We even know intuitively what instrument is
playing.

00:02:45.439 --> 00:02:46.700
Why is that?

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.020
This is called the timbre of a note and different
instruments have different timbres.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:54.840
Pianos and guitars are examples of harmonic
instruments and when they produce a note,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.480
they aren’t just producing a pure note of
a single frequency.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:04.030
Each note is a combination of multiple frequencies
all related to the base note, the fundamental

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.030
frequency.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:09.090
These are called overtones, and they are simply
multiples of the base frequency.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.299
Each instrument has a unique combination and
evolution of these overtones that give it

00:03:13.299 --> 00:03:14.810
that unique sound.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:19.980
Again, it’s quite easy for our brains to
distinguish between a piano and a guitar,

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.849
but we need a way to quantify these characteristics
for a computer to recognise, and this is where

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:26.650
the spectrogram comes in.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.629
A spectrogram is a visual representation of
sound.

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:35.040
It’s a 3D graph with time on the x-axis,
frequency on the y-axis, and the amplitude

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.019
of the frequency, or in other words the loudness,
on the z-axis, which is often represented

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:41.549
by a colour.

00:03:41.549 --> 00:03:46.310
This 3D graph is something a computer can
absolutely recognise and store as data, but

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:51.049
there is huge amount of data within a spectrogram
like this, and the more data there is the

00:03:51.049 --> 00:03:53.709
more computation time is required to find
a match.

00:03:53.709 --> 00:03:58.700
So the first step in reducing computation
time is reducing the data required to classify

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.170
a song.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:03.989
Shazam uses something they call a fingerprint,
where they transform these spectrograms into

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:05.709
something that looks like star map.

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:10.749
[2] Here each star represents the strongest
frequencies at particular times.

00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:15.650
Doing this, we have not only reduced our graph
from 3 dimensions down to 2, but have drastically

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:18.820
reduced the amount of data points on the graph.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:22.000
This is the first vital part of Shazam’s
technology.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.360
Every single song in Shazams database is stored
in a fingerprint like this.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.850
When you open your phone and hit that Shazam
button, the app accesses your microphone and

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:35.310
begins to create its own fingerprint of the
sound waves it receives.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:39.980
This ingenious method also helps the shazam
app to filter out noise because it only creates

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.660
data points for stand-out frequencies.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.091
Once the app has created a fingerprint of
your audio, it then sends it to the shazam

00:04:47.091 --> 00:04:50.750
servers where the recognition part of the
process begins.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:52.259
This is where things get difficult.

00:04:52.259 --> 00:04:56.979
Let’s look at a simplified song fingerprint,
and a recorded fingerprint to see why.

00:04:56.979 --> 00:05:02.730
The recorded fingerprint is only a short recording
of the song, in our example we have just 3

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:07.410
possible frequencies, and each recorded fingerprint
will have just 3 time points.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:11.979
If we want to check the first 3 time points
in the song for a match we first check the

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:17.190
3 frequencies, then we move onto the next
time point and check the 3 possible frequencies

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:20.169
again, and do the same for the final time
point.

00:05:20.169 --> 00:05:25.500
If we find a match, that is 9 operations required
to find a match, but obviously that isn’t

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:26.500
likely.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:31.260
We then need to do those nine operations for
every time point in the song, or perhaps every

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:36.139
time point in Shazams massive music archive,
this obviously is going to take a lot of computation

00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:37.280
time.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.110
This is not how Shazam looks for a match.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.069
First Shazam categorises fingerprints in a
clever way.

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:47.960
We don’t search to see if a note exists
in a song, we search to see if several notes

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:52.270
exist separated by a particular time, just
as brain does.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.539
This becomes our searchable address for a
hash table.

00:05:55.539 --> 00:05:59.720
Hashes and hash functions are an incredibly
useful technique that appear everywhere in

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:01.300
computer science.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:06.360
Hash functions can be found in search algorithms
used by Google, to make sure files are downloaded

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.310
correctly, and are the backbone of crypto
currencies like bitcoin.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:10.310
[3]

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:14.550
A hash function takes a varying length of
input and produces a fixed length output,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:15.699
called a hash.

00:06:15.699 --> 00:06:20.021
In practice, the input can be anything from
a short piece of text, like a password, to

00:06:20.021 --> 00:06:22.879
a long data stream like an entire movie.

00:06:22.879 --> 00:06:24.860
Consider a library of books.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:29.220
We want to store each book on a shelf so we
can find it later, and we know we’ll have

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.810
the title of the book when we’re searching
for it.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:36.250
We can use a hash function to decide which
shelf to put a book on, using the title of

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:40.770
the book as the input and producing a shelf
number as an output.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:45.810
The first goal of a hash function is to produce
outputs that are uniformly distributed...In

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:50.569
our library, we want the books to be spread
evenly across the shelves, so no shelf in

00:06:50.569 --> 00:06:54.700
particular will end up full of books, leaving
others almost empty.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:58.680
The second goal of a hash function is that
it should reduce collisions.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.020
A collision is when two different inputs produce
the same output hash.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:07.550
In our case, a collision results in two or
more books on the same shelf.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:12.510
If our library only has two shelves, collisions
will be really common, no matter what hash

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:13.639
function you use.

00:07:13.639 --> 00:07:17.590
If our library had a billion shelves, a good
hash function will mean collisions will be

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:18.590
rare.

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:21.940
Another goal of a hash function is that it
should be quick to calculate.

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:26.050
If our library has millions of books, we don’t
want to take too long figuring out which shelf

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:27.930
each one needs to be on.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:32.349
A simple hash function might be to take the
title of a book, and group them on shelves

00:07:32.349 --> 00:07:33.650
alphabetically.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.889
This would be really quick to calculate, but
it would result in a lot of collisions, with

00:07:37.889 --> 00:07:42.060
many books on the same shelf, and wouldn’t
be very well distributed.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.759
Think about how many book titles begin with
the word “THE”, compared to how many book

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:47.199
titles start with the letter “Z”.

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:51.650
An alternative might be to take the position
of each letter in the alphabet and sum up

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:53.800
the letters in the book title.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:58.099
We could then divide that number by the number
of shelves we have and take the remainder

00:07:58.099 --> 00:08:00.850
as the shelf number to store the book on.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:05.470
This would still be fairly fast to calculate,
and would prevent all the books with titles

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.620
starting with the word “THE” being stored
on the same shelf.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:13.319
Now imagine, instead of book titles, our hash
function takes data from our two frequencies

00:08:13.319 --> 00:08:19.010
separated by particular time as an input,
t, and produces a number between 1 and...say

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.010
1 billion.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:23.949
First, we go through our database of songs
and calculate the hash number for each anchor

00:08:23.949 --> 00:08:25.419
point.

00:08:25.419 --> 00:08:29.610
Songs will contain multiple anchor points,
which will allow us to categorise short snippets

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:34.140
of songs by the frequency of the anchor point,
the frequency the following point and the

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:35.729
time between them.

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:40.300
And just like the library, we store each anchor
point in order by the hash.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:44.740
These addresses’ are also categorised with
song IDs and time stamps within the song in

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:48.660
a secondary hash table, allowing us to search
for matching songs.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.500
This makes it much faster to locate our matches,
and to find our song we will require multiple

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.230
matching anchor points.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:09:00.410
This ingenious method of song recognition
allowed Shazam to be sold for 400 million

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:04.610
dollars to Apple, and help you figure out
just what that catchy song is.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:09.170
